Okay, let's illustrate inheritance in Java. Inheritance allows a new class (the subclass or derived class) to inherit properties and behaviors (fields and methods) from an existing class (the superclass or base class). This promotes code reusability and establishes an "is-a" relationship.

Here's an example:

```java
// Superclass (Base Class)
class Animal {
    String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void eat() {
        System.out.println(name + " is eating.");
    }

    public void sleep() {
        System.out.println(name + " is sleeping.");
    }
}

// Subclass (Derived Class) inheriting from Animal
class Dog extends Animal {
    String breed;

    public Dog(String name, String breed) {
        super(name); // Call the superclass constructor
        this.breed = breed;
    }

    public void bark() {
        System.out.println(name + " the " + breed + " is barking!");
    }
}

public class InheritanceExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog myDog = new Dog("Buddy", "Golden Retriever");
        myDog.eat();   // Inherited from Animal
        myDog.sleep(); // Inherited from Animal
        myDog.bark();  // Specific to Dog
    }
}
```

In this example, `Dog` *is-a* `Animal`. It inherits the `name`, `eat()`, and `sleep()` from `Animal`. The `Dog` class also adds its own unique property (`breed`) and method (`bark()`).  The `super(name)` call in the `Dog` constructor ensures the `Animal` constructor is executed to initialize the inherited `name` field. This demonstrates how inheritance builds upon existing code, creating specialized classes while retaining common characteristics.

Okay, here's a CSS style designed to create a visually appealing, subtly animated "call to action" (CTA) button. I'll provide the CSS code, followed by a detailed explanation of what each part does and why it's structured the way it is.

```css
.cta-button {
  background-color: #4CAF50; /* Green background */
  border: none;
  color: white;
  padding: 15px 32px;
  text-align: center;
  text-decoration: none;
  display: inline-block;
  font-size: 16px;
  margin: 4px 2px;
  cursor: pointer;
  border-radius: 5px;
  transition: background-color 0.3s ease; /* Smooth transition */
}

.cta-button:hover {
  background-color: #3e8e41; /* Darker green on hover */
}

.cta-button:active {
  background-color: #3e8e41;
  transform: translateY(2px); /* Slight downward movement on click */
  box-shadow: 0px 2px 5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.2); /* Adds shadow on click */
}
```

**Explanation:**

This style defines a class `.cta-button` which can be applied to any HTML button or link element. The `background-color`, `color`, `padding`, and `font-size` properties establish the basic appearance. `text-decoration: none` removes underlines from links. `display: inline-block` allows the button to have padding and margin while remaining inline.  `border-radius` gives it rounded corners. The `cursor: pointer` changes the cursor to a hand icon on hover, signaling interactivity.

The `:hover` pseudo-class changes the background color to a darker shade when the mouse hovers over the button, providing visual feedback.

The `:active` pseudo-class is the key to the animation.  `transform: translateY(2px)` moves the button down by 2 pixels when it's clicked, creating a subtle "pressed" effect. `box-shadow` adds a subtle shadow, enhancing the depth. The `transition` property on the base class ensures that the background color change on hover is smooth and gradual. This combination makes the button feel more responsive and engaging.

```javascript
function reverseWords(str) {
  // 1. Split the string into an array of words
  const words = str.split(" ");

  // 2. Reverse the order of words in the array
  const reversedWords = words.reverse();

  // 3. Join the reversed array back into a string, using spaces
  const reversedString = reversedWords.join(" ");

  // 4. Return the reversed string
  return reversedString;
}

// Example Usage:
const inputString = "This is a sample string";
const reversedString = reverseWords(inputString);
console.log("Original string:", inputString);
console.log("Reversed string:", reversedString); // Output: string sample a is This
```

**Explanation:**

The `reverseWords` function takes a string `str` as input.  First, the `split(" ")` method divides the string into an array of individual words, using a space as the delimiter. For example, "This is a string" becomes `["This", "is", "a", "string"]`.

Next, the `reverse()` method is called on the `words` array. This reverses the order of the elements within the array, so our example array becomes `["string", "a", "is", "This"]`.

Finally, the `join(" ")` method combines the elements of the reversed array back into a single string.  A space is inserted between each word during the joining process. Thus, `["string", "a", "is", "This"]` transforms into "string a is This". The function then returns this newly constructed, reversed string.  This approach efficiently reverses the order of words while preserving the individual words themselves.

